+/* ##########################################################################
+   PAPER-SCRAPS MOCKUP — candidate-3-stretch.css
+   Appended block. A shipping version would fold these overrides
+   into the .df-scrap rules above rather than restating them.
+   ########################################################################## */
+/* ==========================================================================
+   CANDIDATE 3 — "THE STRETCH"                         (mockup, 7 Aug 2026)
+   Paper-scraps art direction. NOT SHIPPED. BUILT TO PRICE IT, NOT TO SHIP.
+   APPLY CANDIDATES 1 AND 2 FIRST — this file is the delta on top of them.
+
+   The tactile half of the north star (`new intro images/Codex Image 4 Aug
+   2026, 16_56_48.png`): ragged torn edges, magenta paper underside showing
+   between and beneath the pieces, hard ink shadows, one lifted curling
+   corner.
+
+   THE MOVE THAT MAKES IT SAFE — the layers are turned inside out.
+   A ragged edge only reads as torn if something shows through the bites, and
+   that something must never be the photograph: a bitten edge over the
+   picture would leak a sliver of the answer along every seam of every scrap
+   before the player had torn anything. So:
+
+       the BUTTON becomes the magenta underside sheet, and it always covers
+       its whole grid cell;
+       the cream PAPER moves onto ::before, and that is what gets clipped.
+
+   A bite in the paper can then only ever expose magenta. This also buys back
+   two things the naive version loses: hit testing stays rectangular (a
+   pseudo-element's clip-path does not affect it), and the :active
+   press-darkening keeps working (the ragged filter now lives on ::before,
+   not on the button).
+
+   WHAT IT STILL COSTS — this is the honest bill:
+     * the paper has to be REBUILT on ::before, which means every background
+       candidate 1 and 2 put on the button is restacked per grid index (the
+       nine rules below). One shared surface, three owners.
+     * ::after is now doing three unrelated jobs across the board: the
+       locked-state dimming style.css already hangs on it, the printer's mark
+       on scrap 8, and the lifted corner on scrap 4 — each needing its own
+       escape hatch from the other two.
+     * nine more composited layers (a drop-shadow per ::before) that
+       recomposite through the .28s tear transition.
+     * the lifted corner is hand-placed on ONE grid index. It has no idea
+       whether that scrap has been torn, or what a second one would look
+       like.
+     * the nine silhouettes are generated static data — 60 points each. No
+       designer can open them, and re-rolling them means re-running a script
+       that does not live in this repo.
+     * clip-path still clips a FOCUS RING. .df-scrap has no keyboard focus
+       style today so nothing breaks, but the day one is added it has to go
+       on the button (rectangular), never on the paper.
+   ========================================================================== */
+
+/* --- The button is now the paper's magenta back ------------------------- */
+.df-scrap {
+  background: linear-gradient(160deg, var(--df-magenta) 0%, #C9269C 100%);
+  border-color: transparent;
+  box-shadow: 1.5px 1.5px 0 rgba(11,11,11,.20);
+}
+/* The dashed affordance moves with it: the dashes are drawn on the button's
+   edge, which is the magenta rim, not the torn paper edge. Deliberate — a
+   dashed line that followed the tear would read as damage rather than as an
+   invitation. Worth a look on a real phone before anyone commits. */
+.df-scrap.tearable { border-color: rgba(11,11,11,.8); }
+
+/* --- The paper moves onto ::before, and gets torn ----------------------- */
+.df-scrap::before {
+  opacity: 1;
+  /* the piece's own ink, cast onto its magenta backing */
+  filter: drop-shadow(1.4px 1.4px 0 rgba(11,11,11,.30));
+}
